SUMMARY:Osamu James Nakagawa: TRACE | Exhibition
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTSTAMP:20260825T085845Z
UID:20261031T040000Z-2393932@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID;VALUE=DATE:20261031
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/11/width/168/height/168/crop/1
 /src_region/0,0,768,1108/32178_Nakagawa-from-trace-WEB.rev.1787661818.jpg
CATEGORIES:Art and museum exhibitions,Lectures, talks, seminars, and confe
 rences
DESCRIPTION:Opening reception and artist talk: Friday\, September 4\, 58 p
 m  Nakagawa combines photographs taken at the sites of Japanese-American i
 ncarceration camps with photograms of found objects and rubbings of the me
 morials.  Using diptychs\, Nakagawa pairs the past and the present\, flatt
 ening the boundaries between them. TRACE seeks to reveal what can't be see
 n anymore\, things that happened eighty years ago and have been quietly bu
 ried. Through a direct and immersive process\, using actual objects and su
 rfaces from the sites\, Nakagawa unearths this history of forced incarcera
 tion.About the artist:  Osamu James Nakagawa was born in New York City in 
 1962\, raised in Tokyo\, and then returned to the US at the age of 15. He 
 is the Distinguished Professor and the Ruth N. Halls Professor of Photogra
 phy at Indiana University. Nakagawa is a recipient of the 2010 Guggenheim 
 Fellowship\, the 2010 Higashikawa New Photographer of the Year and 2015 Sa
 gamihara Photographer of the Year in Japan.  Nakagawa's work has been exhi
 bited internationally\, selected solo exhibitions include PGI\, Tokyo\; Ky
 oto University of the Arts\; Sepia EYE\, New York\; Sakima Art Museum\, Ok
 inawa\, Japan\; and others. Group shows include "A Shared Elegy" with Emme
 t Gowin and Elijah Gowin\; and "After Photoshop" at The Metropolitan Museu
 m of Art\, New York. His work is included in numerous public collections\,
  including George Eastman Museum and the Tokyo Photographic Art Museum.  L
 ink to the artist's website:https://jamesnakagawa.com/en (https://jamesnak
 agawa.com/en)  FAR Center for Contemporary Arts:https://thefar.org/events/
 event/121689 (https://thefar.org/events/event/121689)
LAST-MODIFIED:20260825T131709Z
LOCATION:FAR Center for Contemporary Arts\, 202 S. Rogers St.\, Bloomingto
 n\, IN 47404
URL:https://events.iu.edu/soaadiub/event/2393932-osamu-james-nakagawa-trac
 e-exhibition
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Opening reception and artist talk:
  Friday\, September 4\, 5–8 pm</strong></p><p>  Nakagawa combines photog
 raphs taken at the sites of Japanese-American incarceration camps with pho
 tograms of found objects and rubbings of the memorials.</p><p>  Using dipt
 ychs\, Nakagawa pairs the past and the present\, flattening the boundaries
  between them. TRACE seeks to reveal what can't be seen anymore\, things t
 hat happened eighty years ago and have been quietly buried. Through a dire
 ct and immersive process\, using actual objects and surfaces from the site
 s\, Nakagawa unearths this history of forced incarceration.</p><p><strong>
 About the artist:</strong></p><p>  Osamu James Nakagawa was born in New Yo
 rk City in 1962\, raised in Tokyo\, and then returned to the US at the age
  of 15. He is the Distinguished Professor and the Ruth N. Halls Professor 
 of Photography at Indiana University. Nakagawa is a recipient of the 2010 
 Guggenheim Fellowship\, the 2010 Higashikawa New Photographer of the Year 
 and 2015 Sagamihara Photographer of the Year in Japan.</p><p>  Nakagawa's 
 work has been exhibited internationally\, selected solo exhibitions includ
 e PGI\, Tokyo\; Kyoto University of the Arts\; Sepia EYE\, New York\; Saki
 ma Art Museum\, Okinawa\, Japan\; and others. Group shows include "A Share
 d Elegy" with Emmet Gowin and Elijah Gowin\; and "After Photoshop" at The 
 Metropolitan Museum of Art\, New York. His work is included in numerous pu
 blic collections\, including George Eastman Museum and the Tokyo Photograp
 hic Art Museum.</p><p>  Link to the artist's website:<br /><a href="https:
 //jamesnakagawa.com/en">https://jamesnakagawa.com/en</a></p><p>  FAR Cente
 r for Contemporary Arts:<br /><a href="https://thefar.org/events/event/121
 689">https://thefar.org/events/event/121689</a></p>
