SUMMARY:The Declaration of Independence: The Signers
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261031
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261101
DTSTAMP:20260914T120823Z
UID:20261031T040000Z-2417007@events.iu.edu__20261031
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/261/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,551,551/31917_franklin_button_2.rev.1786550505.jpg
CATEGORIES:Art and museum exhibitions
DESCRIPTION:As part of the America 250 (https://bloomington.iu.edu/campus-
 life/events/america-250/) initiative\, the Lilly Library is proud to prese
 nt an expansion of our Declaration of Independence exhibition\, focusing o
 n the 56 signatories.  IU Libraries Lilly Library invites you to The Decla
 ration of Independence: The Signers\, an exhibition of letters and documen
 ts bearing the autographs of the fifty-six men who signed the August 2\, 1
 776 handwritten copy of the Declaration. Though the length and content of 
 these letters and documents vary widely\, from brief notes or orders to lo
 ng and detailed personal letters\, they all offer insight into the daily l
 ives\, activities\, and concerns of the signers. One aspect of their lives
  that these documents don't reveal\, however\, is the ownership of slaves 
 by a great many of the signers\, which is discussed in the "Race\, Slavery
 \, and Native Americans" section of the exhibition in the Lilly Library's 
 North Gallery\, The Declaration of Independence: The Motives\, The Moment.
  (https://libraries.indiana.edu/lilly-library/declaration-independence)  D
 ocuments signed by fifty-four of the fifty-six signers are displayed in th
 e Lilly Library's South Gallery. Documents signed by the two other signers
 —Philip Livingston of New York and James Smith of Pennsylvania—are on 
 display in the North Gallery in The Declaration of Independence: The Motiv
 es\, The Moment.
GEO:39.167909;-86.518974
LAST-MODIFIED:20261028T142004Z
LOCATION:LILLY LIBRARY
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2379047-the-declaration-of-independe
 nce-the-signers
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  As part of the <a href="https://blooming
 ton.iu.edu/campus-life/events/america-250/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
 >America 250</a> initiative\, the Lilly Library is proud to present an exp
 ansion of our Declaration of Independence exhibition\, focusing on the 56 
 signatories.</p><p>  IU Libraries Lilly Library invites you to <em>The Dec
 laration of Independence: The Signers</em>\, an exhibition of letters and 
 documents bearing the autographs of the fifty-six men who signed the Augus
 t 2\, 1776 handwritten copy of the Declaration. Though the length and cont
 ent of these letters and documents vary widely\, from brief notes or order
 s to long and detailed personal letters\, they all offer insight into the 
 daily lives\, activities\, and concerns of the signers. One aspect of thei
 r lives that these documents don't reveal\, however\, is the ownership of 
 slaves by a great many of the signers\, which is discussed in the "Race\, 
 Slavery\, and Native Americans" section of the exhibition in the Lilly Lib
 rary's North Gallery\, <em><a href="https://libraries.indiana.edu/lilly-li
 brary/declaration-independence" target="_blank" rel="noopener">The Declara
 tion of Independence: The Motives\, The Moment.</a></em></p><p>  Documents
  signed by fifty-four of the fifty-six signers are displayed in the Lilly 
 Library's South Gallery. Documents signed by the two other signers—Phili
 p Livingston of New York and James Smith of Pennsylvania—are on display 
 in the North Gallery in <a href="https://libraries.indiana.edu/lilly-libra
 ry/declaration-independence" target="_blank" rel="noopener"><em>The Declar
 ation of Independence: The Motives\, The Moment</em></a>.</p>
