BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 353": "https://events.iu.edu/iumaa/event/hidden-mythologies-udmurt-traditions-myths"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_353
X-SOURCE:IU Mathers Museum of World Cultures
DTSTART:20261031T180000Z
DTEND:20261031T190000Z
LOCATION:IU Museum of Archaeology and Anthropology\, 416 N. Indiana Ave.\
 , Bloomington\, IN 47408
GEO:39.170023;-86.526132
SUMMARY:Hidden Mythologies: Udmurt Traditions & Myths
DESCRIPTION:Event Details:    Now in its second year\, the Hidden Mytholo
 gieslecture series explores the rich and often overlooked mythological tr
 aditions of the peoples of the Volga-Ural region and beyond. Through stor
 ies of gods\, spirits\, legendary heroes\, sacred landscapes\, and tradit
 ional beliefs\, the series examines the ways mythology reflects and prese
 rves the cultures and worldviews of diverse communities across Eurasia.  
     In this program\, guests will enter the mythological world of the Udm
 urt people and discover figures such as Inmar\, Kyldysin\, Kuaz\, and Ker
 emet (Luda). The lecture also explores traditional amulets and concepts o
 f happiness\, revealing connections between mythology\, belief\, and ever
 yday life. The program concludes with a live performance of the Udmurt fo
 lk song Chagyr\, chagyr dydyke (lullaby) performed by Dariana Antipova.  
   Each program begins with a presentation by Dr. Golshat Safiullina explo
 ring an aspect of the featured culture's mythological traditions\, follow
 ed by Folk Song Club\, an interactive musical experience curated by music
 al director Daryana Antipova\, bringing&#160\;another dimension to the st
 ories\, beliefs\, and cultural heritage explored in the lecture.      Thi
 s event is held in collaboration with the Slavic and East European Langua
 ges Department\, the Jacobs School of Music\, and the CHAKCHAK: Languages
  and Cultures Foundation.  Parking Information:Limitedcomplimentary parki
 ngis available in front of theMuseum entranceoff Indiana Avenue. Street p
 arking is free on weekends and weekdays after 5:00 PM in the neighborhood
 s surrounding the Museum. Paid parking options include theUniversity Coll
 ections at McCallaparking lot off Indiana Avenue\, theIndiana Memorial Un
 ionvisitor lot\, and nearby metered street parking.Convenient metered str
 eet parking is available in the downtown/Sample Gates area along Kirkwood
  Avenue\, East 6th Street\, East 4th Street\, North Lincoln Street\, Nort
 h Dunn Street\, and South Grant Street.Questions?  For accessibility assi
 stance or questions about this event\, please reach out to Camden Hill at
  camrhill@iu.edu (mailto:camrhill@iu.edu) or (812) 855-1696.Note:  By att
 ending this event\, you agree to be in photography that may be used by th
 e University for promotional purposes. If you would not like to be in any
  pictures\, please speak to a Museum staff member and they will inform as
 signed photographers of your request. If you see that your image has been
  used in error\, please reach out to iumaa@iu.edu (mailto:iumaa@iu.edu) t
 o request that it be taken down.
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><strong>Event Details:</strong></p><div c
 lass="ewa-rteLine"><p class="ewa-rteLine">    Now in its <strong>second y
 ear</strong>\, the <strong>Hidden Mythologies</strong><strong>lecture ser
 ies</strong> explores the rich and often overlooked mythological traditio
 ns of the peoples of the Volga-Ural region and beyond. Through stories of
  gods\, spirits\, legendary heroes\, sacred landscapes\, and traditional 
 beliefs\, the series examines the ways mythology reflects and preserves t
 he cultures and worldviews of diverse communities across Eurasia.  </p><p
  class="">    In this program\, guests will enter the mythological world 
 of the <strong>Udmurt people</strong> and discover figures such as <stron
 g>Inmar</strong>\, <strong>Kyldysin</strong>\, <strong>Kuaz</strong>\, an
 d <strong>Keremet (Luda)</strong>. The lecture also explores traditional 
 amulets and concepts of happiness\, revealing connections between mytholo
 gy\, belief\, and everyday life. The program concludes with a live perfor
 mance of the Udmurt folk song <strong><em>Chagyr\, chagyr dydyke</em></st
 rong> (<em>lullaby</em>) performed by <strong>Dariana Antipova.</strong><
 /p><p class="">    Each program begins with a presentation by <strong>Dr.
  Golshat Safiullina</strong> exploring an aspect of the featured culture'
 s mythological traditions\, followed by Folk Song Club\, an interactive m
 usical experience curated by musical director <strong>Daryana Antipova\,<
 /strong> bringing&#160\;another dimension to the stories\, beliefs\, and 
 cultural heritage explored in the lecture.  </p><p class="ewa-rteLine">  
   This event is held in collaboration with the Slavic and East European L
 anguages Department\, the Jacobs School of Music\, and the CHAKCHAK: Lang
 uages and Cultures Foundation.  </p></div><div class="ewa-rteLine"></div>
 <p><strong>Parking Information:</strong></p><p><span data-contrast="auto"
 >Limited</span><span data-contrast="auto">complimentary parking</span><sp
 an data-contrast="auto">is available in front of the</span><a href="https
 ://maps.app.goo.gl/k4Txsojst2gSW5fUA"><span data-contrast="none">Museum e
 ntrance</span></a><span data-contrast="auto">off Indiana Avenue. Street p
 arking is free on weekends and weekdays after 5:00 PM in the neighborhood
 s surrounding the Museum. Paid parking options include the</span><span da
 ta-contrast="none"></span><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/3i62KNcjDeCACr
 uHA"><span data-contrast="none">University Collections at McCalla</span><
 /a><span data-contrast="auto">parking lot off Indiana Avenue\, the</span>
 <span data-contrast="none"></span><a href="https://maps.app.goo.gl/2n49Vo
 LuF78o5twJA"><span data-contrast="none">Indiana Memorial Union</span></a>
 <span data-contrast="auto">visitor lot\, and nearby metered street parkin
 g.</span></p><p><span data-contrast="auto">Convenient metered street park
 ing is available in the downtown/Sample Gates area along Kirkwood Avenue\
 , East 6th Street\, East 4th Street\, North Lincoln Street\, North Dunn S
 treet\, and South Grant Street.</span></p><p data-ogsc="black"><strong>Qu
 estions?</strong></p><p data-ogsc="black">  For accessibility assistance 
 or questions about this event\, please reach out to Camden Hill at <a hre
 f="mailto:camrhill@iu.edu" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">camr
 hill@iu.edu</a> or (812) 855-1696.</p><p data-ogsc="black"><strong>Note:<
 /strong></p><p data-ogsc="black">  By attending this event\, you agree to
  be in photography that may be used by the University for promotional pur
 poses. If you would not like to be in any pictures\, please speak to a Mu
 seum staff member and they will inform assigned photographers of your req
 uest. If you see that your image has been used in error\, please reach ou
 t to <a href="mailto:iumaa@iu.edu">iumaa@iu.edu</a> to request that it be
  taken down.</p>
