BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 11": "https://events.iu.edu/soaadiub/event/2451465-david-bamber-the-invisible-hand-lecture"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_11
SUMMARY:David Bamber: The Invisible Hand | Lecture
DTSTART:20261104T223000Z
DTEND:20261105T000000Z
DTSTAMP:20261014T145821Z
UID:20261104T223000Z-2451465@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261104T223000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/11/width/168/height/168/crop/1
 /src_region/0,0,768,768/33173_TobyMitchell2026_DavidBamber_103_ALT-WEB.rev
 .1792533219.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Widely recognized for his decades-long creative relationship w
 ith Tom Ford and his prominent role within major global fashion houses. Hi
 s career spans high-profile positions in luxury womenswear\, creative dire
 ction\, film\, theater\, and education.  After graduating with first-class
  honors in Fashion and Textiles from Gloucestershire College of Art and De
 sign\, Bamber spent his early career honing his technical skills in Christ
 ian Dior's tailoring and dressmaking workrooms and developing his now reno
 wned eye for detail. Subsequently\, Bamber worked as a costume designer fo
 r production teams both in the U.K. and internationally and as senior desi
 gner with Calvin Klein.  Within five years\, Bamber joined Gucci to rebuil
 d the then-struggling brand. Working under Tom Ford\, Bamber was a major c
 ontributor to the success of the brand and was seconded to both Stella McC
 artney and Yves Saint Laurent. During his tenure at Gucci\, sales increase
 d from 230 million dollars in 1994 to almost 3 billion dollars in 2003\, m
 aking Gucci one of the most profitable brands in the world.  Since leaving
  Gucci in 2006\, Bamber has consulted across fashion\, film\, theater\, an
 d music\, and worked extensively with Tom Ford until 2023.  In this talk\,
  Bamber will share the key pillars of a career that has shaped the history
  of fashion design and luxury brands: trust\, partnership\, education\, di
 scipline\, craftmanship\, taste\, and legacy.The Bill Blass Speaker Series
  was established in 2002 with a bequest from fashion designer Bill Blass\,
  a native Hoosier and friend of Indiana University. The Bill Blass Speaker
  Series is presented and hosted by the Eskenazi School of Art\, Architectu
 re + Design and the College of Arts and Sciences.
GEO:39.165971;-86.523584
LAST-MODIFIED:20261020T215654Z
LOCATION:KIRKWOOD HALL
URL:https://events.iu.edu/soaadiub/event/2451465-david-bamber-the-invisibl
 e-hand-lecture
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p class="p1">  Widely recognized for his dec
 ades-long creative relationship with Tom Ford and his prominent role withi
 n major global fashion houses. His career spans high-profile positions in 
 luxury womenswear\, creative direction\, film\, theater\, and education.</
 p><p class="p1">  After graduating with first-class honors in Fashion and 
 Textiles from Gloucestershire College of Art and Design\, Bamber spent his
  early career honing his technical skills in Christian Dior's tailoring an
 d dressmaking workrooms and developing his now renowned eye for detail. Su
 bsequently\, Bamber worked as a costume designer for production teams both
  in the U.K. and internationally and as senior designer with Calvin Klein.
 </p><p class="p1">  Within five years\, Bamber joined Gucci to rebuild the
  then-struggling brand. Working under Tom Ford\, Bamber was a major contri
 butor to the success of the brand and was seconded to both Stella McCartne
 y and Yves Saint Laurent. During his tenure at Gucci\, sales increased fro
 m 230 million dollars in 1994 to almost 3 billion dollars in 2003\, making
  Gucci one of the most profitable brands in the world.</p><p class="p1">  
 Since leaving Gucci in 2006\, Bamber has consulted across fashion\, film\,
  theater\, and music\, and worked extensively with Tom Ford until 2023.</p
 ><p class="p1">  In this talk\, Bamber will share the key pillars of a car
 eer that has shaped the history of fashion design and luxury brands: trust
 \, partnership\, education\, discipline\, craftmanship\, taste\, and legac
 y.</p><p class="p1"><em>The Bill Blass Speaker Series was established in 2
 002 with a bequest from fashion designer Bill Blass\, a native Hoosier and
  friend of Indiana University. The Bill Blass Speaker Series is presented 
 and hosted by the Eskenazi School of Art\, Architecture + Design and the C
 ollege of Arts and Sciences.</em></p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<div>\n  Yaël Ksander\n</div>\n<div>\n  Director
  of Communications and Marketing\n</div>\n<div>\n  Eskenazi School of Art
 \, Architecture + Design\n</div>\n<div>\n  <a href="mailto:yksander@iu.edu
 ">yksander@iu.edu</a>\n</div>
X-LIVEWHALE-COST:Free
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2451465
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/11/width/168/height
 /168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,768\,768/33173_TobyMitchell2026_DavidBamber_1
 03_ALT-WEB.rev.1792533219.jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE-CAPTION:David Bamber
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p class="p1">\n  In this lecture\, luxury fashion des
 igner David Bamber shares stories from his 50 years in the industry.\n</p>
