SUMMARY:Global Competencies Skills Development Series: Mapping a Refugee C
 amp — Interactive Policy Simulation with Ambassador Gina Abercrombie-Win
 stanley
DTSTART:20261105T150000Z
DTEND:20261105T170000Z
DTSTAMP:20260803T132911Z
UID:20261105T150000Z-2368472@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261105T150000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/135/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,2000,2000/31509_GLOBAL_COMPETENCIES_SKILLS_DEVELOPMENT_SE
 RIES.rev.1784660853.png
CATEGORIES:Career development,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences,T
 raining, non-credit classes, and learning opportunities
DESCRIPTION:Real Crisis. Real Decisions. Real Tradecraft.  When displaceme
 nt crises hit\, how do international leaders manage logistics\, security\,
  human rights\, and geopolitical pressure in real time?  Skip the standard
  lecture and get your hands on actual diplomatic decision-making. Join the
  Global Competencies Skills Development Series for Mapping a Refugee Camp
 \, an interactive\, fast-paced crisis response simulation led by Ambassado
 r Gina Abercrombie-Winstanley—former U.S. Ambassador to Malta\, 30-year 
 Foreign Service veteran\, and President of the Middle East Policy Council.
   Having led emergency war-zone evacuations and served across Baghdad\, Ca
 iro\, and Jeddah\, Ambassador Abercrombie-Winstanley will guide you throug
 h the complex tradecraft\, resource constraints\, and tactical choices req
 uired on the front lines of global humanitarian crisis management.  Who Ca
 n Attend? Open to all: Students (Undergrad &amp\; Grad)\, Faculty\, Staff
 \, and the Public. (Light snacks and drinks provided)  &#160\;  What You'l
 l Get Out of It      Hands-On Tactical Experience: Practice real-world dec
 ision-making under competing geopolitical\, logistical\, and ethical press
 ures.          Direct Professional Access: Work through crisis scenarios d
 irectly alongside a senior diplomat who operated across the State Departme
 nt\, Defense Department\, and National Security Council.          Resume-B
 uilding Tradecraft: Build core skills in crisis navigation\, strategic neg
 otiation\, and rapid resource allocation—essential for careers in foreig
 n policy\, intelligence\, NGOs\, and national security.          Active Ne
 tworking: Team up with peers\, faculty\, and practitioners across campus o
 ver light refreshments.      Bio: Ambassador Gina Abercrombie-Winstanley  
 Ambassador Gina Abercrombie-Winstanley is a 30-year diplomat\, former U.S.
  Ambassador to the Republic of Malta\, and President of the Middle East Po
 licy Council. Her career spans executive leadership roles across the Depar
 tment of State\, Defense Department\, National Security Council\, and Sena
 te Foreign Relations Committee. She has advised the commander of U.S. cybe
 r forces\, expanded international counterterrorism partnerships\, and led 
 the emergency evacuation of thousands of U.S. citizens from a war zone. Sh
 e is a recipient of the State Department Superior Honor Award for acts of 
 courage during an al-Qaeda attack on the U.S. Consulate in Jeddah.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20260810T184818Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/hls/event/2368472-global-competencies-skills-dev
 elopment-series
