X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 130": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2394294-easc-colloquium-adrift-in-the-south-xiao-hai-tony-ha"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_130
SUMMARY:EASC Colloquium: Adrift in the South - Xiao Hai\, Tony Hao
DTSTART:20261105T180000Z
DTEND:20261105T193000Z
DTSTAMP:20260914T120801Z
UID:20261105T180000Z-2416931@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261105T180000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/140/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,500,755/32188_9781738536269.rev.1787673008.jpg
CATEGORIES:Culture and languages,Lectures, talks, seminars, and conference
 s
DESCRIPTION:When Xiao Hai turned fifteen\, his family paid a vocational-sc
 hool teacher 1\,200 yuan to find him a factory job in Shenzhen. So began f
 ifteen years spent moving between the garment mills and electronics factor
 ies of China's fast-growing southern cities.Adrift in the South is a memoi
 r of life as a migrant labourer in the twenty-first century\, making iPhon
 es and baby clothes\, hand-stitching football shirts and cutting plastic i
 nto radios. Here\, Xiao Hai reveals the alienation and tedium of factory l
 ife\, the small indignities and indifference of the larger system. And he 
 tells the story of how poetry led him somewhere unexpected: to join a smal
 l community of artists living\, working\, and studying together on the out
 skirts of Beijing.  This memoiris a landmark text from China's migrant wor
 ker literature movement\, a grassroots group of writers providing an unvar
 nished account of what life is like for the 300 million migrant workers po
 wering the world's second-biggest economy.About the author: Xiao Hai was b
 orn in Shangqiu City in Henan Province in China. At fifteen he moved to Sh
 enzhen to join the workforce\, moving between cities and factory jobs whil
 e writing poetry in his spare time. He is the author of the poetry collect
 ion Sisyphus on the Wenyu River\, and translations of his poetry have appe
 ared in World Literature Today and Chinese Literature Today.About the tran
 slator: Tony Hao is a New Haven\, Connecticut-based Chinese-to-English lit
 erary translator and writer. Authors he has translated include Taiwanese n
 ovelist Tong Wei-Ger 童偉格\, Chinese worker-poet Xiao Hai 小海\, Tai
 wanese transgender writer Liu Chen-Chun 劉宸君\, and Chinese short stor
 y writer Ban Yu 班宇.  His debut book-length translation\, Adrift in the
  South\, a memoir by Xiao Hai\, was published by Granta Magazine Editions 
 in May\, 2026.  His short-form translations have appeared in Granta\, The 
 Wall Street Journal\, The Georgia Review\, The Offing\, Books from Taiwan
 \, Florescence (The Taipei Chinese PEN)\, MAYDAY Magazine\, and elsewhere.
  His work has been recognized by the PEN Translates Grant\, the Granum Fou
 ndation Translation Prize\, Art Omi Writers: Translation Lab\, the Vermont
  Studio Center\, BCLT's Advanced Translation Workshop\, the Bread Loaf Tra
 nslators' Conference\, and elsewhere.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20261001T130551Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2394294-easc-colloquium-adrift-in-th
 e-south-xiao-hai-tony-ha
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  When Xiao Hai turned fifteen\, his famil
 y paid a vocational-school teacher 1\,200 yuan to find him a factory job i
 n Shenzhen. So began fifteen years spent moving between the garment mills 
 and electronics factories of China's fast-growing southern cities.</p><p><
 i>Adrift in the South </i>is a memoir of life as a migrant labourer in the
  twenty-first century\, making iPhones and baby clothes\, hand-stitching f
 ootball shirts and cutting plastic into radios. Here\, Xiao Hai reveals th
 e alienation and tedium of factory life\, the small indignities and indiff
 erence of the larger system. And he tells the story of how poetry led him 
 somewhere unexpected: to join a small community of artists living\, workin
 g\, and studying together on the outskirts of Beijing.</p><p>  This memoir
 <i></i>is a landmark text from China's migrant worker literature movement
 \, a grassroots group of writers providing an unvarnished account of what 
 life is like for the 300 million migrant workers powering the world's seco
 nd-biggest economy.</p><p><strong>About the author:</strong> Xiao Hai was 
 born in Shangqiu City in Henan Province in China. At fifteen he moved to S
 henzhen to join the workforce\, moving between cities and factory jobs whi
 le writing poetry in his spare time. He is the author of the poetry collec
 tion <i>Sisyphus on the Wenyu River</i>\, and translations of his poetry h
 ave appeared in <i>World Literature Today</i> and <i>Chinese Literature To
 day</i>.</p><p class="" data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>About the t
 ranslator:</strong> Tony Hao is a New Haven\, Connecticut-based Chinese-to
 -English literary translator and writer. Authors he has translated include
  Taiwanese novelist Tong Wei-Ger 童偉格\, Chinese worker-poet Xiao Hai 
 小海\, Taiwanese transgender writer Liu Chen-Chun 劉宸君\, and Chines
 e short story writer Ban Yu 班宇.</p><p class="" data-rte-preserve-empty
 ="true">  His debut book-length translation\, <em>Adrift in the South</em>
 \, a memoir by Xiao Hai\, was published by Granta Magazine Editions in May
 \, 2026.</p><p class="" data-rte-preserve-empty="true">  His short-form tr
 anslations have appeared in <em>Granta</em>\, <em>The Wall Street Journal
 \, The Georgia Review\, The Offing</em>\, <em>Books from Taiwan\, Floresce
 nce (The Taipei Chinese PEN)\, MAYDAY Magazine</em>\, and elsewhere. His w
 ork has been recognized by the PEN Translates Grant\, the Granum Foundatio
 n Translation Prize\, Art Omi Writers: Translation Lab\, the Vermont Studi
 o Center\, BCLT's Advanced Translation Workshop\, the Bread Loaf Translato
 rs' Conference\, and elsewhere.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-CONTACT-INFO:<p>\n  easc@iu.edu\n</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2416931
X-LIVEWHALE-IMAGE:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/140/width/168/heigh
 t/168/crop/1/src_region/0\,0\,500\,755/32188_9781738536269.rev.1787673008.
 jpg
X-LIVEWHALE-RSVP:1
X-LIVEWHALE-SUMMARY:<p>\n  EASC presents a reading with author Xiao Hai of
  his new memoir <em>Adrift in the South\,</em> followed by a conversation 
 with Xiao Hai and translator Tony Hao.<br />\n  <br />\n  <strong>When:</s
 trong> Thursday November 5<br />\n  <strong>Time:</strong> 1:00 pm<br />\n
   <strong>Where:&#160\;</strong>GISB room 1060<em><br /></em>\n</p>
