BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"WFIU Community Calendar": "https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/event/2026-poynter-lecture-kelly-mcbride-27-10-2026-11-28-45"}
X-SOURCE-ID:wfiu_community_calendar
SUMMARY:2026 Poynter Lecture: Kelly McBride
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261110T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261110T183000
UID:98562a64cddf2e6e99d46ce73f40748d@ipm.org
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims4/default/68ca
 5cb/2147483647/strip/true/crop/1200x1200+0+0/resize/254x254!/quality/90/?u
 rl=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2Fe3%2F4b%2Fb05273134ab287
 eac58ed377e87c%2Fkelly-mcbride.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Public Media is Dead! Long Live Public Media!\n Kelly McBride
 \, senior vice president at The Poynter Institute of Media Ethics and chai
 r of the Newmark Ethics Center at Poynter.\n When Congress voted to kill t
 he Corporation for Public Media\, some saw it as a death blow for public m
 edia in America. And it may yet be\, especially for local news\, which is 
 90 percent of public media. In spite of the massive outpouring of support
 \, the system faces significant challenges if it is going to survive. And 
 yet\, there is an amazing opportunity to rethink and remake public media i
 n the image of what consumers and communities need now\, in the era of str
 eamers and influencers. But can we do it? Will we get it right? And will i
 t last?\n BIO: As part of her work\, Kelly McBride guides professional new
 s organizations to develop best practices in the service of people and dem
 ocracy. Since 2020 she has served as the public editor for NPR\, where she
  handles audience questions about NPR’s journalistic credibility. She’
 s addressed accusations of liberal bias\, questions about NPR’s coverage
  of President Donald Trump and concerns about Nina Totenberg’s friendshi
 ps with individual Supreme Court justices. In the past\, she filled a simi
 lar role for ESPN. McBride is a co-editor of The New Ethics of Journalism:
  Guiding Principles for the 21st Century\, and author of several articles 
 on media ethics for The New York Times\, BBC\, CNN\, and Washington Post.
LOCATION:Presidents Hall in Franklin Hall\, 601 E. Kirkwood Ave.\, Bloomin
 gton\, Indiana 47405
URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/event/2026-poynter-lecture-kell
 y-mcbride-27-10-2026-11-28-45
X-SOURCE:WFIU Community Calendar
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"WFIU Community Calendar": "https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/event/2026-poynter-lecture-nprs-kelly-mcbride-27-10-2026-16-36-16"}
X-SOURCE-ID:wfiu_community_calendar
SUMMARY:2026 Poynter Lecture: NPR's Kelly McBride
DTSTART;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261110T170000
DTEND;TZID=America/Indiana/Indianapolis:20261110T183000
UID:7f6fcee51d9cee345542084cd6f2de6b@ipm.org
ATTACH;FMTTYPE=image/jpeg:https://npr.brightspotcdn.com/dims4/default/191a
 6b3/2147483647/strip/true/crop/1142x856+0+0/resize/254x190!/quality/90/?ur
 l=http%3A%2F%2Fnpr-brightspot.s3.amazonaws.com%2F1a%2Fb4%2F7195c2ab4e3eb7e
 f3c520436d847%2Fmcbride.jpg
DESCRIPTION:Presented by Indiana Public Media and The Media School at Indi
 ana University\nKelly McBride\, senior vice president at The Poynter Insti
 tute of Media Ethics and chair of the Newmark Ethics Center at Poynter.\n 
 When Congress voted to kill the Corporation for Public Media\, some saw it
  as a death blow for public media in America. And it may yet be\, especial
 ly for local news\, which is 90 percent of public media. In spite of the m
 assive outpouring of support\, the system faces significant challenges if 
 it is going to survive. And yet\, there is an amazing opportunity to rethi
 nk and remake public media in the image of what consumers and communities 
 need now\, in the era of streamers and influencers. But can we do it? Will
  we get it right? And will it last?\n As part of her work\, Kelly McBride 
 guides professional news organizations to develop best practices in the se
 rvice of people and democracy. Since 2020 she has served as the public edi
 tor for NPR\, where she handles audience questions about NPR’s journalis
 tic credibility. She’s addressed accusations of liberal bias\, questions
  about NPR’s coverage of President Donald Trump and concerns about Nina 
 Totenberg’s friendships with individual Supreme Court justices. In the p
 ast\, she filled a similar role for ESPN. McBride is a co-editor of The Ne
 w Ethics of Journalism: Guiding Principles for the 21st Century\, and auth
 or of several articles on media ethics for The New York Times\, BBC\, CNN
 \, and Washington Post.
LOCATION:Presidents Hall in Franklin Hall\, 601 E. Kirkwood Ave.\, Bloomin
 gton\, Indiana 47405
URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/event/2026-poynter-lecture-nprs
 -kelly-mcbride-27-10-2026-16-36-16
X-SOURCE:WFIU Community Calendar
X-SOURCE-URL:https://www.ipm.org/community-calendar/
END:VEVENT
