 90348979.jpg
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:Buildings and cities\, Mindrup argues\, endure not by remainin
 g unchanged but by repeatedly relinquishing what they once were.  Mindrup 
 is an architect\, scholar\, and associate professor of architecture at the
  University of Sydney\, where he directed the multidisciplinary Bachelor o
 f Architecture and Environments from 2019 to 2025. He is a senior fellow o
 f the Higher Education Academy and has taught architecture at institutions
  in the United States\, Switzerland\, Canada\, and Australia.  His forthco
 ming book\, "Architecture Forgets: The Art and Tactics of Transformation" 
 (MIT Press\, 2026)\, turns from how architecture comes into being to how i
 t changes over time.The J. Irwin Miller Architecture Program Lecture and E
 xhibition Series brings prominent working artists and architects to Columb
 us. Lectures take place in the auditorium and exhibitions in the gallery\,
  both located within The Republic Building\, 333 Second Street in Columbus
 . The gallery is open Monday through Friday from 9 a.m. to 5 p.m. All even
 ts are free and open to the public.
GEO:39.199929;-85.921713
LAST-MODIFIED:20260925T152153Z
LOCATION:The Republic Building
URL:https://events.iu.edu/soaadiub/event/2430809-matthew-mindrup-architect
 ure-forgets-an
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p class="p1">  Buildings and cities\, Mindru
 p argues\, endure not by remaining unchanged but by repeatedly relinquishi
 ng what they once were.</p><p class="p1">  Mindrup is an architect\, schol
 ar\, and associate professor of architecture at the University of Sydney\,
  where he directed the multidisciplinary Bachelor of Architecture and Envi
 ronments from 2019 to 2025. He is a senior fellow of the Higher Education 
 Academy and has taught architecture at institutions in the United States\,
  Switzerland\, Canada\, and Australia.</p><p class="p1">  His forthcoming 
 book\, "Architecture Forgets: The Art and Tactics of Transformation" (MIT 
 Press\, 2026)\, turns from how architecture comes into being to how it cha
 nges over time.</p><p class="p1"><em>The J. Irwin Miller Architecture Prog
 ram Lecture and Exhibition Series brings prominent working artists and arc
 hitects to Columbus. Lectures take place in the auditorium and exhibitions
  in the gallery\, both located within The Republic Building\, 333 Second S
 treet in Columbus. The gallery is open Monday through Friday from 9 a.m. t
 o 5 p.m. All events are free and open to the public.</em></p>
