X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2388026-tobias-center-research-seminar-organizing-developmen"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:Tobias Center Research Seminar: Organizing Developmental Statecraf
 t
DTSTART:20261113T170000Z
DTEND:20261113T183000Z
DTSTAMP:20261016T093900Z
UID:20261113T170000Z-2454056@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261113T170000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/185/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,1080,1350/33130_16OCT26_Hong_Zhang_Flyer.rev.1792163835.p
 ng
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:This installment of the Tobias Center research seminar series 
 features Hong Zhang\, Assistant Professor in the IU Department of Internat
 ional Studies. Her talk will explore her upcoming book project\, Organizin
 g Developmental Statecraft: Nodal Bureaucratic Coordination in China's Glo
 bal Economic Strategy.Abstract:&#160\;China is both a rising power interna
 tionally and a developing economy confronting persistent domestic developm
 ent challenges\, in particular regional inequality and fragmentation. This
  book project argues that China's efforts to build structural power in the
  global economy cannot be properly understood apart from its continuing pr
 oject of domestic developmental transformation. I develop the concept of d
 evelopmental statecraft to describe state efforts to couple these two proc
 esses: advancing domestic developmental objectives while shaping the exter
 nal economic networks through which development takes place. Developmental
  statecraft thus links the integration of markets\, infrastructure\, produ
 ction\, and finance across domestic and international scales.  How is deve
 lopmental statecraft organized? This talk focuses on the nodal bureaucrati
 c coordination through China's National Development and Reform Commission 
 (NDRC). I argue that the NDRC acts as a nodal agency connecting policy dom
 ains and actors that are otherwise institutionally fragmented. Through pro
 grammatic planning\, interministerial coordination\, central-local linkage
 s\, project gate-keeping\, and international agenda alignment\, these inst
 itutional processes help produce strategic coherence between domestic deve
 lopmental agendas and external economic strategy. The analysis identifies 
 bureaucratic coordination capacity as a critical dimension of developmenta
 l statecraft and shows how the organization of development can itself beco
 me a source of structural power in the global political economy.  Lunch wi
 ll be provided.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20261016T151727Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/2388026-tobias-center-research-semin
 ar-organizing-developmen
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p>  This installment of the Tobias Center re
 search seminar series features Hong Zhang\, Assistant Professor in the IU 
 Department of International Studies. Her talk will explore her upcoming bo
 ok project\, <em>Organizing Developmental Statecraft: Nodal Bureaucratic C
 oordination in China's Global Economic Strategy.</em></p><p><strong>Abstra
 ct:</strong>&#160\;China is both a rising power internationally and a deve
 loping economy confronting persistent domestic development challenges\, in
  particular regional inequality and fragmentation. This book project argue
 s that China's efforts to build structural power in the global economy can
 not be properly understood apart from its continuing project of domestic d
 evelopmental transformation. I develop the concept of developmental statec
 raft to describe state efforts to couple these two processes: advancing do
 mestic developmental objectives while shaping the external economic networ
 ks through which development takes place. Developmental statecraft thus li
 nks the integration of markets\, infrastructure\, production\, and finance
  across domestic and international scales.</p><p>  How is developmental st
 atecraft organized? This talk focuses on the nodal bureaucratic coordinati
 on through China's National Development and Reform Commission (NDRC). I ar
 gue that the NDRC acts as a nodal agency connecting policy domains and act
 ors that are otherwise institutionally fragmented. Through programmatic pl
 anning\, interministerial coordination\, central-local linkages\, project 
 gate-keeping\, and international agenda alignment\, these institutional pr
 ocesses help produce strategic coherence between domestic developmental ag
 endas and external economic strategy. The analysis identifies bureaucratic
  coordination capacity as a critical dimension of developmental statecraft
  and shows how the organization of development can itself become a source 
 of structural power in the global political economy.</p><p>  Lunch will be
  provided.</p>
X-LIVEWHALE-ID:2454056
