BEGIN:VEVENT
X-SOURCE-URLS:{"Events Livewhale 135": "https://events.iu.edu/live/events/21jpsi-speaker-series-zack-cooper"}
X-SOURCE-ID:events_livewhale_135
SUMMARY:21JPSI Speaker Series "The U.S.' Shifting Asia Strategy: Implicati
 ons for U.S. Allies" (Dr. Zack Cooper (AEI/Princeton Univ)))
DTSTART:20261117T210000Z
DTEND:20261117T221000Z
DTSTAMP:20261007T133324Z
UID:20261117T210000Z-2443027@events.iu.edu
RECURRENCE-ID:20261117T210000Z
ATTACH:https://events.iu.edu/live/image/gid/162/width/168/height/168/crop/
 1/src_region/0,0,840,1080/32935_20261117_21JPSI_ZACK_COOPER_FLYER.rev.1791
 390446.png
CATEGORIES:Lectures, talks, seminars, and conferences
DESCRIPTION:This public event is open to all students\, faculty\, staff\, 
 and members of the community.Co-sponsored by the Institute for Korean Stud
 ies and Department of Political Science. Please join us!  —————
 ——————–About the talk: Fifteen years ago\, the United States
  promised to "pivot" to Asia. But today Washington continues to be distrac
 ted by events in the Middle East and beyond. As Washington adjusts its app
 roach to Asia\, U.S. allies and partners are starting to rethink their own
  strategies\, as are policymakers in Beijing. How will U.S. policy evolve 
 in the years ahead\, and what implications will this have for Japan and ot
 her U.S. allies in Asia?About the speaker:Zack Cooper is a senior fellow a
 t the American Enterprise Institute\, where he studies US strategy in Asia
 \, including alliance dynamics and US-China competition. He also teaches a
 t Princeton University\, serves as chair of the board of the Open Technolo
 gy Fund\, and co-hosts the Net Assessment podcast.&#160\;Before joining AE
 I\, Dr. Cooper was the senior fellow for Asian security at the Center for 
 Strategic and International Studies. He previously worked as codirector of
  the Alliance for Securing Democracy and senior fellow at the German Marsh
 all Fund of the United States and research fellow at the Center for Strate
 gic and Budgetary Assessments. He also served on staff at the National Sec
 urity Council and in the Office of the Secretary of Defense.  Dr. Cooper i
 s the author of Tides of Fortune: The Rise and Decline of Great Militaries
  from Yale University Press. He has also co-authored books\, reports\, and
  articles on US strategy and alliances in Asia. His writing has appeared i
 n academic journals and popular press\, including International Security\,
 Security Studies\, Foreign Affairs\, the New York Times\, the Washington P
 ost\, and the Wall Street Journal\, among other outlets. Dr. Cooper gradua
 ted from Princeton University with a PhD and an MA in security studies and
  an MPA in international relations. He received a BA in public policy from
  Stanford University.Prof. Adam LIFF (host/moderator) is Professor of East
  Asian International Relations at Indiana University's Hamilton Lugar Scho
 ol of Global and International Studies\, where he also serves as Founding 
 Director of its 21st Century Japan Politics and Society Initiative ("21JPS
 I"). Beyond IU\, he is a Nonresident Senior Fellow with the Center for Asi
 a Policy Studies at the Brookings Institution\, as well as a Faculty Assoc
 iate and Associate-in-Research at Harvard University's Program on U.S.-Jap
 an Relations and Reischauer Institute of Japanese Studies.&#160\;His resea
 rch examines contemporary international security and foreign policy challe
 nges in East Asia—with a particular focus on Japanese politics and forei
 gn policy\; the U.S.-Japan security alliance\; U.S. allies and Taiwan\; U.
 S. Asia-Pacific strategy\; and U.S.-Japan-China relations. He holds a Ph.D
 . and M.A. in Politics from Princeton University\, a postgraduate research
  certificate in International Politics from the University of Tokyo\, and 
 a B.A. in East Asian Studies and Psychology from Stanford University&#160
 \;This event is made possible thanks to generous support from the Japan Fo
 undation.All 21JPSI events are 100% in-person unless otherwise noted.
GEO:39.170002;-86.517492
LAST-MODIFIED:20261026T160353Z
LOCATION:GLOBAL &amp\; INTERNATIONAL STUDIES BUILDING
URL:https://events.iu.edu/live/events/21jpsi-speaker-series-zack-cooper
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<p><em>This public event is open to all stude
 nts\, faculty\, staff\, and members of the community.</em><em>Co-sponsored
  by the Institute for Korean Studies and Department of Political Science. 
 Please join us!</em></p><p>  ————————————–</p><p
  dir="ltr"><span data-olk-copy-source="MessageBody"><strong><span style="t
 ext-decoration: underline\;">About the talk:</span></strong> Fifteen years
  ago\, the United States promised to "pivot" to Asia. But today Washington
  continues to be distracted by events in the Middle East and beyond.</span
 > As Washington adjusts its approach to Asia\, U.S. allies and partners ar
 e starting to rethink their own strategies\, as are policymakers in Beijin
 g. How will U.S. policy evolve in the years ahead\, and what implications 
 will this have for Japan and other U.S. allies in Asia?</p><p><strong><spa
 n style="text-decoration: underline\;">About the speaker</span>:</strong><
 strong>Zack Cooper</strong> is a senior fellow at the American Enterprise 
 Institute\, where he studies US strategy in Asia\, including alliance dyna
 mics and US-China competition. He also teaches at Princeton University\, s
 erves as chair of the board of the Open Technology Fund\, and co-hosts the
  Net Assessment podcast.&#160\;Before joining AEI\, Dr. Cooper was the sen
 ior fellow for Asian security at the Center for Strategic and Internationa
 l Studies. He previously worked as codirector of the Alliance for Securing
  Democracy and senior fellow at the German Marshall Fund of the United Sta
 tes and research fellow at the Center for Strategic and Budgetary Assessme
 nts. He also served on staff at the National Security Council and in the O
 ffice of the Secretary of Defense.</p><p>  Dr. Cooper is the author of <em
 >Tides of Fortune: The Rise and Decline of Great Militaries</em> from Yale
  University Press. He has also co-authored books\, reports\, and articles 
 on US strategy and alliances in Asia. His writing has appeared in academic
  journals and popular press\, including <em>International Security\,</em><
 em>Security Studies</em>\, <em>Foreign Affairs</em>\, the <em>New York Tim
 es</em>\, the <em>Washington Post</em>\, and the <em>Wall Street Journal</
 em>\, among other outlets. Dr. Cooper graduated from Princeton University 
 with a PhD and an MA in security studies and an MPA in international relat
 ions. He received a BA in public policy from Stanford University.</p><p><s
 trong>Prof. Adam LIFF (host/moderator)</strong> is Professor of East Asian
  International Relations at Indiana University's Hamilton Lugar School of 
 Global and International Studies\, where he also serves as Founding Direct
 or of its 21st Century Japan Politics and Society Initiative ("21JPSI"). B
 eyond IU\, he is a Nonresident Senior Fellow with the Center for Asia Poli
 cy Studies at the Brookings Institution\, as well as a Faculty Associate a
 nd Associate-in-Research at Harvard University's Program on U.S.-Japan Rel
 ations and Reischauer Institute of Japanese Studies.&#160\;His research ex
 amines contemporary international security and foreign policy challenges i
 n East Asia—with a particular focus on Japanese politics and foreign pol
 icy\; the U.S.-Japan security alliance\; U.S. allies and Taiwan\; U.S. Asi
 a-Pacific strategy\; and U.S.-Japan-China relations. He holds a Ph.D. and 
 M.A. in Politics from Princeton University\, a postgraduate research certi
 ficate in International Politics from the University of Tokyo\, and a B.A.
  in East Asian Studies and Psychology from Stanford University&#160\;</p><
 p><em>This event is made possible thanks to generous support from the Japa
 n Foundation.</em></p><p><em>All 21JPSI events are 100% in-person unless o
 therwise noted.</em></p>
